The 15 minute time is longer than secure sites such as banks will    
give; we do not force a refresh on pages as 'truly' secure sites will 
(except in our testbank).  But I agree, if you really want to do all  
of a set of exercises (which often includes going browsing on the    
internet and other things) while "sitting in" the page itself, it is  
not nearly enough time to do all that before security times-out!    

I will give you suggestions as "workarounds" I would use were I in    
your situation.  Some are more complex than others but all will work  
 pretty well.  There is no reason to lose data you have input.  It    
only disappears if you don't go back and get it.    

However, before I give you these ideas I have to bore you a bit: as   
our User Agreement and other notes emphasize, work of quality    
*should* be done on your own computer so you have a copy of your own  
work, and so you can use the advantage of a basic word processor (or  
at least a larger space than a little box!) to check spelling and    
other things that affect the quality of work.    

In the end you are responsible for your schoolwork. Any of the up-to- 
 30 internet links between your ISP and the webserver may go down,    
your ISP could go down or disconnect you, you could lose power,    
something in your environment could cause a power or keystroke issue, 
 your software could freeze up, your security could time out -- there 
  is a long list of reasons you should have your own copy of your   
work.  No matter what thing "could" happen to affect your submission  
 or its  receipt, you are responsible to your instructor for your   
work.  So, a  backup -- for your own reference -- is a good  idea.  

But here are some ideas for dealing with system time-out issues.   

If you time-out, when you press the submit button it will take you to 
 the login page.  If you are at that point, do one of two things:    

1. Go ahead and log in.  Then use your browser's "back" key to    
quickly go back a few times to the exercises page you were sitting    
in, which should still be in your browser's cache (should include all 
 your typing).  Press the submit button again. That should work.   

2.  Better: Ignore the transfer to the login page, and go "back" to  
the page you were in by using your browser's back key.  Then press  
Ctrl+N (or   File / New) to open a new browser window.  In THAT  
second/new  browser  window, go to the login page (bookmark this for  
ease, or just hit submit on that one and it'll send you there).  Log  
 in.  That  sets the values for all windows.  Switch back into your  
Q&A page and press the submit button again.  That should work. 

3.  Keep a second browser window open, say on your student home page, 
 while you work in an exercises page.  Say you have 15 questions to   
 answer.  Every time you finish answering a question, click on the    
other page and press Ctrl+R (or the "refresh/reload" button).  This   
 will "update" all your browser windows timeout (reset it to 0). As   
 long as no single question takes more than 15 minutes, this would    
work fine, and would keep your in-page connection live for working on 
 pop-up interactivites, etc.   

4.  As a more complicated approach, which will prohibit use of    
interactivities during this but would open up to indefinite your time 
 in-page otherwise, you could use either MS I.E. or Netscape to    
"bookmark/save for offline viewing" a given area of the website.   

This would probably save an entire unit at a time just FYI; don't    
choose to save more than 1 level, no off-server links, etc. (or it'll 
 take eons to save it all). (Also, you'd need to do this in a browser 
  program, not Acrobat/Word, because you'll need to be connected to   
 browser cookie values that come when you login later.)  Then you    
could go to the page offline and spend as long as you wanted in it... 
   
...When you are ready to submit, open a second browser window and log 
 into the website with it, and then go back to your first browser    
window with the saved page, and press the submit button.  Because the 
 value that is set with login will apply to all your browser windows  
  that should also work fine.   

Hope this helps!   

